            values." A checked-in artefact stating outright that its predecessor's geometry was drawn by hand, not
            measured.
          </p>
          <p>
            A2 — the expressive treatment of the same conditions data as A3, a radar of eleven spokes — is where that
            shift showed most. In v1, spoke radii were placed by eye: dramatic, asymmetric, a shape that read as two
            visibly different cohorts. In v2, every vertex radius is bound exactly to the reproduced percentage — r(p%)
            = p/100 × 150px, the same rule for both polygons. Bound to the real numbers, the two cohort shapes nearly
            coincide. The chart became more honest and less visually interesting. It was demoted, and it did not ship.
          </p>
          <p class="bp-note">
            This is a statement about a visual-design process, not a population claim — it describes what happened to
            one chart's geometry when its radii stopped being chosen and started being computed, not what the underlying
            respondents do or don't have in common.
          </p>
